Subject: [PATCH] A new block is checked again once it is in the jump table: a write just before is not lost

FillBlock64 builds a block with its x86 code protected and, at its end, checks that the code
stayed protected and the same. internalDBGetBlock puts the block into the jump table only after
that. Another thread's write to the page in between faults, and the fault (unprotectDB) marks the
blocks it finds through the jump table, which do not include this one yet, and lifts the
protection. The block then went into the jump table clean, on a page that was writable again:
a later write to its code raised no fault and marked nothing, and the block stayed with code that
was no longer there.

.NET writes a method's precode once (call PrecodeFixupThunk becomes a jump to the compiled code)
and from then on sends callers to the precode. A precode whose block went in this way, and that was
patched while its page was still writable, kept its old block: every thread that called the method
ran the old precode, and the prestub found the method compiled and sent it back to the precode, for
ever, at a full core. The official PD2 launcher stopped so at its start, before its window showed.
A diagnostic build found a block put into the jump table clean on a page whose protection a write
had lifted in about a third of the launcher's starts, most of them precodes.

Once the block is in the jump table, a later write marks it, so it is checked again then: on a page
that is no longer protected, or code that changed, it is marked (its jump-table entry becomes
jmpnext), and its next run validates it with its code protected, or builds it again.
---
--- a/src/dynarec/dynablock.c
+++ b/src/dynarec/dynablock.c
@@ -437,7 +437,8 @@
     // check size
     if(block) {
         // fill-in jumptable
-        if(!addJumpTableIfDefault64((void*)addr, (block->dirty || block->always_test)?block->jmpnext:block->block)) {
+        void* jmp = (block->dirty || block->always_test)?block->jmpnext:block->block;
+        if(!addJumpTableIfDefault64((void*)addr, jmp)) {
             FreeDynablock(block, 0, 0);
             block = getDB(addr);
             MarkDynablock(block);   // just in case...
@@ -451,6 +452,19 @@
                 }
                 block->done = 1;    // don't validate the block if the size is null, but keep the block
                 rb_inc(my_context->db_sizes, block->x64_size, block->x64_size+1);
+                // FillBlock64 built the block with its code protected and checked that the code
+                // stayed protected and the same, but the block goes into the jump table only now.
+                // Another thread's write in between faulted, and the fault marked the blocks of
+                // the jump table only (not this one) and lifted the protection: the block would
+                // stay there clean on a writable page, and a later write to its code would mark
+                // nothing (.NET patches a method's precode once; a thread that then runs the old
+                // precode calls the prestub for ever). Look again now that a later write marks the
+                // block: on an unprotected page or a change, mark it, so its next run validates it.
+                if(jmp==block->block && (!isprotectedDB((uintptr_t)block->x64_addr, block->x64_size)
+                    || X31_hash_code((void*)block->x64_readaddr, block->x64_size)!=block->hash)) {
+                    dynarec_log(LOG_INFO, "Block %p at %p written before it went into the jump table: marked\n", block, block->x64_addr);
+                    MarkDynablock(block);
+                }
             }
         }
     }
